* Competing after the opponents have bid twice (NOTES.md #P12)

Phase 4 (lane D2). The booklet's competitive section stops at the first round:
it says how to overcall and double an opening, not what to do once the opener's
partner has raised, or when the opponents stop low after a fit. This file writes
the standard practice (the booklet's own methods, adjusted by the level, and the
"balancing" rule that one does not let the opponents play at the two level
when they have found a fit and stopped there):

- A double is for takeout, short in their suit; a suit bid is natural.
- In the direct seat, after a raise to the three level, both need an opening
  hand (13+) and a six-card suit at the four level.
- In the balancing seat (their raise, then two passes) the double and a suit
  bid can be about a king lighter, since partner is marked with some values.
- Opposite a balancing or competitive double, advancer bids the cheapest
  unbid suit with four cards (a major first; three cards of a minor when
  there is nothing else), 3NT with a stopper and values, game in a major
  with four cards and 10+, and passes for penalty with five good trumps.

Every one of these calls ends our bidding (the doubler does not bid again),
and every table ends in a system stop: with any other hand we pass, and keep
passing while they bid on.

The tables use the phase-4 relative levels (`cM` = a major at the cheapest
level, `jM` one level higher; docs/design/06-system.md §4.6), so one table
serves every suit the opponents can have raised.

